# XSH times()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# The page leaves the accuracy of the times unspecified, so the tests ask for
# the effect of about half a second of work to show as at least one tick, and
# no more. A time value that cannot be compared because clock_t wrapped, or a
# times() that fails with EOVERFLOW (allowed), makes the test UNRESOLVED.

assertion  XSH.times.001
anchor     functions/times.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     functions/times.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       times() returns elapsed real time in clock ticks from a fixed
           reference: two calls 1.5 s apart (by CLOCK_MONOTONIC, around a
           nanosleep) differ by between 0.5 s and 6 s worth of ticks
           ({CLK_TCK} from sysconf(_SC_CLK_TCK)), and a call in between
           lies between them.
note       The page states no accuracy; the bounds are generous (a third
           to four times the interval) and would only catch a wrong unit.
test       t/001-elapsed.c

assertion  XSH.times.002
anchor     functions/times.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       tms_utime counts CPU time spent in user instructions: after the
           process has used about 0.5 s of CPU time in a loop of
           arithmetic (clock() as the measure), tms_utime has grown by at
           least one tick.
test       t/002-utime.c

assertion  XSH.times.003
anchor     functions/times.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       tms_stime counts CPU time the system spends for the process:
           after about 0.5 s of CPU time spent mostly in system calls
           (many one-byte writes to /dev/null), tms_stime has grown by at
           least one tick.
test       t/003-stime.c

assertion  XSH.times.004
anchor     functions/times.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/times.html#RATIONALE
class      required
since      1988
says       When waitpid() returns a terminated child, tms_cutime and
           tms_cstime include its time: after a child that used about 0.5 s
           of CPU time, their sum has grown by at least one tick.
test       t/004-child-included.c

assertion  XSH.times.005
anchor     functions/times.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/times.html#RATIONALE
class      required
since      1988
says       Before the parent has waited for it, a terminated child's time is
           not in tms_cutime or tms_cstime: for a child that has ended but
           not been waited for, both are still 0.
test       t/005-not-before-wait.c

assertion  XSH.times.006
anchor     functions/times.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       If a child has not waited for its own children, their times are
           not in its times: a child whose own child used about 0.5 s of
           CPU time and ended, but was not waited for, reads tms_cutime and
           tms_cstime as 0.
test       t/006-grandchild-not-included.c

assertion  XSH.times.007
anchor     functions/times.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/times.html#RATIONALE
class      required
since      1988
says       Child times are added up through the generations: when a child
           has waited for its own child (0.5 s of CPU), its tms_cutime plus
           tms_cstime is at least one tick, and after the parent has waited
           for that child the parent's tms_cutime plus tms_cstime is at
           least what the child had seen of its own plus its own
           tms_utime plus tms_stime at its last reading.
test       t/007-recursive.c
